// SDL3 — windows, rendering, events, input and audio, bound to the library the machine has.
//
// This is the block a consumer writes to reach this package:
//
//     dependencies {
//       sdl3 { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/sdl3", version = "0.2.5" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate is identity rather than a URL, so it carries no `https://`, and the version is the
// tag `v0.2.5` on this repository.
//
// **SDL3 has to be installed, and so do its headers.** Nothing is vendored here: SDL is a large C
// project with its own build system and its own platform backends, and a package that carried it
// would be carrying a port rather than a binding.
//
//     brew install sdl3              # macOS
//     sudo apt install libsdl3-dev   # Debian / Ubuntu
//
// **A build needs the prefix on the command line**, at both ends, because a toolchain searches its
// own directories and Homebrew's is not one of them:
//
//     sysl run prog.sysl --include-path sdl3=/opt/homebrew/include --link-path /opt/homebrew/lib
//
// The headers are read because `sh.sysl.sdl3.c` asks the C compiler for SDL's own constants rather
// than transcribing them. That is deliberate and is not a gap: `15 §8` refuses both a `@link_path`
// attribute and a `package.hocon` field for a prefix, because where one lives is a fact about
// somebody's laptop rather than a property of the package. `LIBRARY_PATH` and `CPATH` work too,
// since clang reads them, and are the better answer on a machine where the setting never changes.
//
// **The companion libraries are separate packages, and that is forced rather than chosen.** A link
// directive is never pruned (`LinkDirectives`: "nothing is pruned"), so a single package holding
// the text, image and mixer bindings would put `-lSDL3_ttf -lSDL3_image -lSDL3_mixer` on the link
// line of every program that used any of it — and a machine with only SDL3 installed could not link
// a program that only draws rectangles. So: `sdl3-ttf`, `sdl3-image` and `sdl3-mixer` each depend
// on this one and each name their own library.

package {
  name    = "sdl3"
  version = "0.2.5"
}

// **`posix`, because SDL is a hosted library.** It opens devices, starts threads, talks to a window
// server; there is no freestanding target this works on, and saying so turns "this cannot work
// there" into one refusal rather than a link error naming `SDL_Init`.
//
// **`heap`, because the binding allocates.** A `CString` copied for every C call that takes text,
// the buffer a vertex array is built in, the handle every `&T` sits behind — all of it is sysl
// storage with a lifetime, which is what keeps this side from ever having to free what SDL handed
// back.
//
// `posix` implies `os`, so that one is not written out.
requires {
  heap  = true
  posix = true

  // `sh.sysl.sdl3.c` asks the C compiler for SDL's constants rather than transcribing them, so the
  // headers are read at compile time and not only at link time — and `@link("SDL3")` needs the
  // library itself at the end of it. Both are one fact, *this machine must have SDL3 installed*, and
  // SDL answers it itself: an `sdl3.pc` sits beside the library wherever it came from, so the machine
  // is asked rather than the reader.
  //
  // **The module is `sdl3` and the link directive says `SDL3`, which is why the name is written here
  // rather than derived from it.** `-lSDL3` and `sdl3.pc` are two naming conventions that happen to
  // share a word, and nothing recovers one from the other.
  //
  // This replaces the `headers { sdl3 = … }` this file carried until 0.2.1, and does not sit beside
  // it: the probe deliberately does not satisfy a header requirement, so declaring both would refuse
  // every build that did not pass the flag the key exists to make unnecessary. It also retires the
  // sentence about giving the directory *above* SDL3 — pkg-config knows which one it is, and that
  // instruction existed because a reader could not be expected to.
  //
  // `--include-path sdl3=<dir>` still answers it and stops the probe, for a machine with no
  // pkg-config.
  pkg_config {
    sdl3 = "SDL3 — `brew install sdl3` on macOS, Debian's libsdl3-dev, and pkg-config finds it either way"
  }
}
